{"body":"Adds a bounded hosted-dev HTTP load test and a manual performance workflow for the course performance/scalability demonstration. The runner reuses three authenticated sessions, ramps through 1/10/25/50/100 VUs with three-minute steady windows, drives read-heavy group/chat/Archive journeys, and caps photo contributions at three tiny files. It retains per-request JSON and p50/p95/p99, throughput, and error summaries; each steady stage must meet p95 < 2 seconds and errors < 1%.\n\nThe workflow accepts `target_url` and `max_vus`, uses SHA-pinned actions, read-only repository permissions and an artifact upload that runs on failure. A Python analyzer creates measured/projected tables and three 1280×720 presentation charts. Empty collecting-state archives are counted separately from issued media capabilities. A separate confirmation mode reuses private fixtures, runs only the maximum-VU stage and performs reads without account creation, uploads or chat writes.\n\nHosted evidence: the original 16-minute mixed-workload run passed through 50 VUs. At 100 VUs, p95 was 1,368 ms but errors were 10.88%, so the result is FAIL. All 339 failures fell within the installation step of [dev deployment 40341542790](https://github.com/Collaboration95/rewind-app/actions/runs/40341542790). The failed run is retained. A separate three-minute post-deployment read-only 100-VU confirmation passed with p95 625 ms and zero errors. Capacity projections are labelled as incident-influenced and do not establish physical host saturation. Presentation artifacts were delivered separately.\n\nValidation: `npm ci`, `npm run test:root`, `npm run test:fast`, focused harness regression, Prettier and ESLint, and the full pre-push checks. The analyzer recovered known parameters in a synthetic numerical check; all three final PNGs are 1280×720, and JSON evidence was scanned for session-token leakage.\n\nNo merge or deployment is requested.\n","files":[{"path":".github/workflows/performance.yml","additio